      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 4250 mg three times daily comes to 12,750 mg/day, which is five times the maximum recommended metformin dose of about 2,550 mg/day (2,000 mg/day for extended-release). A single 4,250 mg dose is also far above the usual 1,000 mg per-dose ceiling. At that exposure the risk of severe lactic acidosis is high, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThis may be a typo. Did you mean one of these?\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day and the standard maximum.\n- **425 mg PO three times daily**, if you wanted a lower dose.\n- **1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n\nTell me the dose you intend and I'll enter it right away. If 4,250 mg TID is deliberate, tell me the clinical rationale and I'll proceed as you direct. I'd still suggest confirming it with pharmacy first."
      }
